WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.960
My whole life changed
with five simple words

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.480
delivered casually
by a relative at a party:

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.240
"You know you're autistic, right?"

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.640
Initially, I rejected the idea.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.920
I was 30 and my knowledge of autism
was based on TV caricatures and digs

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.920
from people who suggested
anyone behaving poorly

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.200
must be "on the spectrum".

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.000
I thought autistic people were
math wizards who lacked empathy

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.200
and loved trains.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.600
Of course, there's no one way
to be autistic,

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.280
and autism can present
quite differently

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:36.720
in different people.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.160
I'm a loudmouth creative
with an overactive empathy chip

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.000
who can barely add two digits
together.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.760
And I couldn't care less
about trains.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.640
My story is more common
than you'd think.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.960
Growing up, my autistic meltdowns -

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.520
a totally involuntary response
to emotional or physical overload,

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.120
were dismissed as attention seeking.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.160
This was really confusing and scary
as I came to believe

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.520
I was somehow acting out on purpose.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:13.080
Add to that sensory sensitivities,
making many lights, noises, smells,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.440
textures and tastes overwhelming
and painful,

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.160
as well as severe aversions
to almost all food

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.240
and "spoilt brat" was added
to the charge sheet.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.000
My tendency to simply blurt out
uncomfortable truths

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:32.000
was misinterpreted by others
as manipulative.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.400
It seemed that everything I did
was misunderstood,

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.560
and so I began
to over-explain myself,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.240
a trait others found annoying.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.400
By the time I was seven,
my self-esteem began to plummet.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:50.520
I tried to hide my difficulties
and differences by disguising

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.480
my obsessive interests or choosing
similar ones to the other girls,

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.320
like boybands, for example -
in my case Hanson,

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.400
even if I never did marry Zac.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.160
This is called masking.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.240
And it's something
many autistic girls do

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:05.880
to try to blend in.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.800
Unfortunately, the long-term effects
of masking

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:12.600
can cause mental health issues
and autistic burn-out,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.920
an experience lots
of autistic people can relate to,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.520
where our difficulties
are suddenly magnified

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.160
and it becomes impossible
to participate in the world.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.200
Autism is often presented
as an illness to be cured,

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.560
when in fact it's just a different
way of experiencing the world.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.880
While it comes with its downsides,
it also has benefits.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.960
Contrary to stereotypes,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.760
autistic people can be creative,
emotional and highly empathetic,

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.640
kind while saying
exactly what we mean.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.200
I care deeply about social justice.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.760
I connect with animals.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.200
I get untold joy
from my intense interests.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:57.600
And when my sensory sensitivities
are focused on positive experiences

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.920
like music, it can be wonderful.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.000
Around one in 100 people in the UK
are thought to be autistic,

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:09.840
with three times as many boys
as girls being diagnosed.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.760
Like many areas of medical research,
women are under-represented

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.800
in studies and many
of the diagnostic criteria

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.040
for autism were developed
with men in mind.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.560
Over the past decade,
our understanding of how autism

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.200
can manifest differently in girls
and women

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.360
and also how our autistic behaviours
can be interpreted differently

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.440
has changed.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.920
But research still has
a long way to go.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.200
Until I was diagnosed as autistic,

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.200
I thought I was a broken,
terrible person.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.120
My diagnosis gave me a framework
to explain my experience

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.600
to loved ones and the wider world.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.360
Meeting other autistic people
gave me permission to understand

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.520
and even begin to like myself.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.080
Being autistic isn't all roses
and sunshine.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.840
Like anything, it comes
with its pros and cons.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.200
Some people see autism
as an illness,

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.000
whilst others call it a superpower.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.760
To me, it's neither.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.120
It's simply the blueprint
for who I am,

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.640
and to cure me of my autism
would be to cure me of myself.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.320
My hope is that society comes
to better understand

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:18.920
the autistic experience

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.640
so that future generations
of women are able to be diagnosed

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.360
at an earlier age.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.920
That way they can be given
the support they need to thrive,

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.560
have self-confidence, and relate
to the world on their own terms

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.920
as their unique, brilliant
autistic selves.

